# Deploy workflow

When the user says **deploy** (or “ship”, “release”, “push to prod”), run this end-to-end. Do not ask for confirmation of the steps unless something is blocked (auth, conflicts, dirty secrets).

## 1. Version bump

- Read `package.json` `version` (semver `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`).
- Default: bump **PATCH** (`1.0.0` → `1.0.1`).
- If the user says **minor** / **major**, bump that segment and reset lower ones.
- Write the new version back to `package.json` only (footer picks it up via `scripts/set-env.mjs` on build).

## 2. Commit

- `git status`, `git diff`, and recent `git log` first (follow the repo commit protocol).
- Stage relevant project changes **and** `package.json`. Never stage `.env`, `environment.generated.ts`, or secrets.
- Commit with a message like: `release: vX.Y.Z` (include a short why if other feature commits are included).
- Only create the commit as part of deploy when the user asked to deploy (that counts as an explicit commit request for this workflow).

## 3. Push to main

- Ensure branch is `main` (or merge/rebase onto `main` if the user is on a feature branch and asked to deploy — prefer staying on `main` for this app).
- `git push -u origin HEAD` to `origin` (`https://github.com/safalttct/MyAlibi.git`).
- Do **not** force-push to `main`.

## 4. Vercel

- Production deploys from **GitHub → Vercel** on push to `main` (`vercel.json` + env vars `NG_APP_SUPABASE_*`).
- After a successful push, tell the user Vercel will build production automatically.
- If `vercel` CLI is available and the project is linked, you may also run `vercel --prod` and report the URL; otherwise do not install the CLI unless asked.

## 5. Reply

Report: new version, commit hash/message, push result, and that Vercel production deploy was triggered (or the CLI URL if used).
